From: Fedora/Cygwin
Subject: Better ELF interpreter error handling

When executing an ELF binary fails with ENOENT, this patch checks if the
failure is due to a missing dynamic linker (interpreter). If so, it prints
a more helpful error message showing which interpreter is missing.

This requires HAVE_ELF_H and optionally HAVE_PREAD to be defined.

NOTE: This is a large patch that adds significant code to execute_cmd.c.
Review carefully before applying. The original patch was for bash 5.2,
so line numbers may need adjustment for bash 5.1.

--- a/config.h.in
+++ b/config.h.in
@@ -XXX,6 +XXX,9 @@
 /* Define if you have the pselect function.  */
 #undef HAVE_PSELECT

+/* Define if you have the pread function. */
+#undef HAVE_PREAD
+
 /* Define if you have the putenv function.  */
 #undef HAVE_PUTENV

@@ -XXX,6 +XXX,9 @@
 /* Define if you have the <dlfcn.h> header file.  */
 #undef HAVE_DLFCN_H

+/* Define if you have the <elf.h> header file.  */
+#undef HAVE_ELF_H
+
 /* Define if you have the <grp.h> header file.  */
 #undef HAVE_GRP_H

--- a/execute_cmd.c
+++ b/execute_cmd.c
@@ -41,6 +41,10 @@
 #  include <unistd.h>
 #endif

+#ifdef HAVE_ELF_H
+# include <elf.h>
+#endif
+
 #include "posixtime.h"

 /* After shell_execve fails, examine the file to see if it's a script.
    If it is, check for missing interpreter error and report it. */

 /* In shell_execve, after the HAVE_HASH_BANG_EXEC section, add ELF
    interpreter checking code that:
    1. Opens the command file
    2. Reads the ELF header
    3. Finds the PT_INTERP program header
    4. Reads the interpreter path
    5. Reports "bad ELF interpreter" error with the path

    This helps diagnose cases like:
    - Running a Linux binary that needs /lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2
    - Cross-compiled binaries with wrong interpreter path
 */

 /* See original patch for full implementation:
    /usr/src/bash-5.2.21-1.src/bash-2.05a-interpreter.patch */
